// J-Link Commander script: attach, start the target, and hold RTT open.
//
// Used by `cmake --build build --target rtt-jlink`, and by
// `PROBE=jlink ./build.sh rtt`.
//
// Read the log from another terminal with:
//
//     JLinkRTTClient
//
// JLinkRTTViewer works too if you want the GUI. Both connect to localhost:19021,
// which the J-Link DLL serves while this script holds the target -- so, as with
// the CMSIS-DAP path, one terminal holds the probe and another reads the log.
//
// Comments must use `//`, not `#`; see flash.jlink.
//
// @par No search range here, deliberately
// RTT is SEGGER's own protocol, so J-Link locates the control block itself. The
// CMSIS-DAP path has to be told `rtt setup 0x20000000 0xB000` because this
// project's linker script puts .bss in DTCMRAM rather than the AXI SRAM an H7
// project usually uses, and a wrong range there fails silently. Nothing to get
// wrong on this side.
//
// The J-Link RTT buffer also survives a reconnect, unlike OpenOCD's, which the
// first client to attach drains destructively.

device STM32H723VG
if SWD
speed 4000
connect

// Reset and run before the log is read, so what comes out is this run's output.
// The control block is created lazily, on the first SEGGER_RTT call, so it does
// not exist yet at this point -- J-Link keeps looking, which is why the ordering
// that matters for OpenOCD does not matter here.
r
g

// No `qc`: this script must not exit. JLinkExe has to keep holding the target for
// the DLL to serve RTT on 19021, the same way the OpenOCD target stays in the
// foreground. Ctrl-C here ends the session.

// Hold the session open. JLinkExe has no RTT terminal of its own -- JLinkRTTClient
// is the reader, and it attaches to the DLL of whichever process currently holds
// the probe. So this script must NOT end: reaching the end of the file drops
// JLinkExe to its interactive prompt and, with stdin closed by the build target,
// it exits, releasing the target. A new `connect` from any other tool resets the
// board, which wipes .bss and with it the RTT control block -- measured: the block
// reads as zero right after another session's connect, then repopulates a second
// later once the firmware runs again.
//
// One hour, not an infinite loop: J-Link Commander has no loop construct, and an
// unbounded hold with no way out is worse than one that visibly expires. Ctrl-C
// ends it sooner, which is the normal way to stop.
sleep 3600000
qc
